WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
Secrets rarely appear in only one obvious place.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.760
A learner might look for a variable named password, but real workflows are broader.

3
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:23.900
Sensitive values can be stored in shell scripts, configuration files, shell profiles, .env files, inventory files, deployment manifests, CI/CD variable stores, container runtime settings, local key files, or service configuration fragments.

4
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.240
Some of those locations are expected.

5
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.620
Others appear because a team needed something to work quickly and never returned to clean up the pattern.

6
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.500
Secrets can also appear in places that were never meant to store them.

7
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:40.000
Command-line arguments may be visible to process inspection tools.

8
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.460
Environment variables may be inherited by child processes.

9
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.280
Shell history may preserve typed commands.

10
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.260
Debug traces may print expanded commands.

11
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.080
Logs may capture headers, connection strings, or derived values.

12
00:00:54.180 --> 00:01:05.700
Temporary files, build artifacts, support bundles, crash reports, and copied terminal output may preserve sensitive material long after the script has finished.

13
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.860
The practical skill is secret flow mapping.

14
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:22.240
Ask where the secret enters the workflow, where it is stored, which process receives it, which child processes inherit it, what files are created, what logs are produced, and how long each copy remains available.

15
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:29.140
That mapping does not need to be elaborate for every small script, but it should be good enough to reveal obvious exposure paths.

16
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:35.480
If nobody can explain where the value goes, the script is probably carrying more risk than the team understands.

17
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:43.200
This map is especially important when scripts connect local machines, CI/CD systems, deployment targets, and cloud services.

18
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:50.180
Each system has different rules for masking, retention, access control, and artifact storage.

19
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:57.080
A defensive engineer does not rely on a single assumption such as, it is in a protected variable store, so it must be safe.

20
00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:03.340
The question is what happens after the variable is injected into the job and how the workflow prevents it from spreading.

21
00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:11.600
Mapping the secret flow reveals where values enter, which processes inherit them, what files and logs are created, and how long copies remain available.
